// A file's `export { … };` manifest is its surface, and a name leaves its file only through it. The
// `import` form always enforced that; a QUALIFIED spelling did not, so the same symbol was reachable by
// naming it the long way:
//
//     import std::collections::{BTreeNode};                  // error: does not export `BTreeNode`
//     std::collections::BTreeNode<int32, int32> n;           // ACCEPTED, before this rule
//
// `lib/std/collections/sorted_map.kama` says `export { SortedMap };` — the B-tree node is the map's
// internal structure, and this is the loophole that let one be stored outside the module that owns it.
//
// Visibility is per FILE, not per module: a sibling in the same directory module is judged by the same
// rule, because `export` is what a name leaves its file through. What `visibility` in `kama.json` governs
// is reach BEYOND the module — see design/module-system.md §2c.
import { std::collections::SortedMap };

type resource Keeper {
    std::collections::BTreeNode<int32, int32> node;
    public ctor make(std::collections::BTreeNode<int32, int32> node) { this.node = give node; }
}

fn int32 main() { return 0; }
